  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBermuda High Pressure will continue to persist towards the\nEastern Seaboard through the remainder of the week and into the\nupcoming weekend, allowing for warm and somewhat humid\nconditions. High temperatures today will mainly be into the \nmiddle to upper 80s before temperatures approach the lower 90s \nThursday through Sunday for areas south of the mountains and \naway from the immediate coast. Convection may limit temperatures\nthough by Saturday and Sunday and therefore Thursday and Friday\nlook to be the warmest days of the stretch. Further to the \nnorth, high temperatures will be into the 80s each day. \n\nDew points will remain relatively low today into the upper 50s\nto lower 60s before rising into the middle to upper 60s Thursday\nthrough Saturday with a few readings possibly approaching the 70\ndegree mark. It is important to note though that often times in \nour area we either mix down drier air aloft to reach the 90s or \nwe don't mix as much and our daytime highs tend to be on the \ncooler side. In this situation, it appears more likely that we \nwill warm well into the 80s to perhaps low 90s in spots but dew \npoints may remain on the lower side of the current forecast from\nthe National Blend of Models (NBM). Nevertheless, afternoon \nheat indices may approach Heat Advisory criteria for some \nsouthern locations by late week. Nighttime lows look to be on \nthe warmer side but not as warm as previous hot spells this \nSummer with most locations falling into the 60s each night. \n\nA series of fronts will cross this weekend into early next week\nwith a return to cooler temperatures as we approach the middle\nof August. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION... \n\nPersistent southwest flow will dominate the pattern the \nremainder of the week as a persistent ridge sets up over the \nwestern Atlantic with a broad trough to our west. This is going \nto bring about elevated dewpoints into the upper 60s to around \n70F, and with heating during the day this should be sufficient \nto generate enough instability for showers and thunderstorms. \nThe latest run of the NBM (04.19Z) is suggesting high \nprobabilities (60-90%) of 500 J/kg of CAPE each afternoon with \nglobal ensembles suggesting this could also be accompanied by \n25-30 kts of shear. The limiting factor is going to be the lack \nof forcing most days which is going to keep any storm \ndevelopment limited in coverage. The best chance for any more \nwidespread showers and storms looks to be Saturday as models \ncontinue to bring a cold front through the area. The finer \ndetails are going to have to wait until we get closer in time, \nbut for now expect the usual summer pattern where it won't hurt \nto have an umbrella or raincoat on hand in the afternoon.\n\nGlobal models are in good agreement that the 500 mb pattern \ntransitions to more zonal flow early next week which may help to\nlevel off temperatures and dewpoints, but weather-wise we \nremain unsettled.",
